# FPMS-OS kernel/bootloader freeze.
#
# Installed by the overlay copy in scripts/50-overlay.sh; verified, moded and
# completed by scripts/70-update-policy.sh. Do not edit on a running rover
# without reading docs/UPDATING.md first - the escape hatch is documented and
# it is not "delete this file".
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THIS PREVENTS, WHICH IS NOT HYPOTHETICAL
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This image is built on Joshua Riek's ubuntu-rockchip Ubuntu 22.04 arm64.
# Its kernel is a Rockchip BSP kernel and it is the ONLY reason this hardware
# works:
#
#   rknpu     the NPU driver. No rknpu, no /sys/kernel/debug/rknpu, no
#             librknnrt, no YOLO - and the agent DEGRADES SILENTLY when the
#             NPU is missing ("NPU unavailable; streaming without detection"),
#             so systemd still reports active and the rover just stops
#             detecting fires.
#   mali      the GPU stack.
#   bcmdhd    the WiFi chip driver. No bcmdhd, no association, no FPMS_Net,
#             no MQTT, no rosbridge - the rover is simply unreachable, on a
#             board with no monitor attached.
#
# STOCK UBUNTU KERNELS CONTAIN NONE OF THESE. A routine `apt upgrade` that
# pulls a generic linux-image-* therefore produces a rover with no NPU, no
# WiFi and possibly no boot, and there is no console on which to see it
# happen. The same is true of the bootloader packages: u-boot-* postinsts on
# this platform rewrite the raw boot sectors of the boot device.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY A PIN AND NOT ONLY `apt-mark hold`
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# scripts/70-update-policy.sh sets holds too, but a hold is not the mechanism
# this file exists to be. A hold is a dpkg-level SELECTION FLAG living in
# /var/lib/dpkg/status:
#
#   - `apt-get --ignore-hold`, `apt-get -o APT::Ignore-Hold=1` and
#     `apt --allow-change-held-packages` all walk straight past it;
#   - it is state, not configuration, so it does not survive a rootfs that is
#     restored, re-flashed from a base image, or debootstrapped;
#   - nothing in the repo would recreate it.
#
# A pin file is part of the IMAGE. It is copied in by the overlay every build,
# it is in git, and apt honours it for `apt-get install`, `upgrade`,
# `full-upgrade`, `dist-upgrade` AND for unattended-upgrades, which resolves
# through the same engine. --ignore-hold does not touch it. That asymmetry is
# the whole design: the hold is a courtesy to the operator (it makes
# `apt-mark showhold` list the protected set); the pin is the guarantee.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY `Pin: release *` AND NOT `Pin: version *` - THE LOAD-BEARING DETAIL
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# apt_preferences(5) splits records into two kinds and resolves them in a
# fixed order:
#
#   specific form   `Pin: version ...`   - matched FIRST
#   general form    `Pin: release ...`   - matched only if no specific-form
#                                          record matched that version
#
# scripts/70-update-policy.sh writes /etc/apt/preferences.d/fpms-kernel-installed
# containing one SPECIFIC-form record per installed kernel/bootloader/BSP
# package, pinning the EXACT version this image shipped with at priority 1000.
# Because specific beats general regardless of filename order, the result is:
#
#   the version this image was built with   ->  1000   (the candidate)
#   every other version of those packages   ->    -1   (never installable)
#
# If this stanza used `Pin: version *` it would be a specific-form record too,
# it would race the generated file on filename order ("fpms-kernel" sorts
# before "fpms-kernel-installed"), the -1 would win, and the INSTALLED kernel
# would end up with `Candidate: (none)`. That is why the pin type here is
# `release` and not `version`. Do not "simplify" it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE FILENAME HAS NO EXTENSION, DELIBERATELY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# apt reads a file in preferences.d only if it ends in `.pref` or has NO
# extension at all. Anything else is skipped, and for the endings in
# Dir::Ignore-Files-Silently (`.disabled`, `.bak`, `~`, `.dpkg-*`) it is
# skipped in silence. So:
#
#   - never rename this to fpms-kernel.conf. It would stop working and say
#     nothing.
#   - `sudo mv fpms-kernel fpms-kernel.disabled` IS the documented, reversible
#     way to switch the freeze off for one deliberate upgrade. See
#     docs/UPDATING.md.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE PACKAGE NAMES
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These are GLOBS, not exact names, and that is on purpose: the exact names in
# the base image cannot be read out of this repository (the base .img is not a
# text file anyone can grep). The glob set does not have to know whether the
# flavour is called `linux-image-5.10.160-rockchip` or `linux-image-rockchip`
# - it blocks the whole family either way, including the generic Ubuntu
# kernels that are the actual danger and that are NOT installed today.
#
# The exact names that ARE installed are discovered at build time by
# scripts/70-update-policy.sh (`dpkg-query`), recorded in
# /etc/apt/preferences.d/fpms-kernel-installed, printed into the build log,
# and cross-checked with `apt-get -s dist-upgrade --ignore-hold`, which proves
# THIS FILE alone holds the line with every dpkg hold ignored.

# --- the kernel ------------------------------------------------------------
# linux-image-*/headers/modules covers both the BSP flavour and every generic
# Ubuntu kernel. linux-generic*/linux-virtual*/linux-lowlatency* are the
# METApackages: they are how a generic kernel arrives as a dependency of
# something innocuous rather than as a direct install.
Explanation: FPMS-OS: the BSP kernel is the only reason rknpu, mali and bcmdhd exist on this board.
Package: linux-image-* linux-headers-* linux-modules-* linux-generic* linux-virtual* linux-lowlatency* linux-rockchip* linux-*-rockchip*
Pin: release *
Pin-Priority: -1

# --- the bootloader --------------------------------------------------------
# u-boot-* on this platform is not a passive package: its postinst writes
# idbloader/u-boot.itb to raw sectors of the boot device, and u-boot-menu
# regenerates /boot/extlinux/extlinux.conf. Either one, done unattended and
# wrongly, is a board that does not come back - with no console to say why.
# flash-kernel is here for the same reason on the images that use it.
Explanation: FPMS-OS: bootloader postinsts write raw sectors; a bad one is an unbootable board with no console.
Package: u-boot* flash-kernel*
Pin: release *
Pin-Priority: -1

# --- the Rockchip BSP userspace and firmware -------------------------------
# libmali*/mali-* are the vendor GPU blobs, matched to the BSP kernel's
# driver. *rockchip* catches rockchip-multimedia-config, librockchip-mpp and
# friends. *bcmdhd* is speculative - see UNVERIFIED below - and costs nothing
# if no such package exists.
#
# linux-firmware is here for a specific reason: the WiFi blobs this board
# needs are not in Ubuntu's linux-firmware, so the base image supplies them,
# and if it supplies them at paths linux-firmware also owns then an upgrade of
# linux-firmware replaces them with Ubuntu's versions and the WiFi stops. That
# provenance is UNVERIFIED from this repository (see docs/UPDATING.md); the
# cost of freezing it is a firmware update this rover does not need, and the
# cost of being wrong the other way is a rover that never joins the hotspot.
Explanation: FPMS-OS: vendor GPU/NPU/WiFi userspace and firmware, version-matched to the BSP kernel.
Package: *rockchip* libmali* mali-* *bcmdhd*
Pin: release *
Pin-Priority: -1

Explanation: FPMS-OS: may own the bcmdhd blobs this board's WiFi needs. UNVERIFIED - see docs/UPDATING.md.
Package: linux-firmware
Pin: release *
Pin-Priority: -1
